┌─────────────────────────────────┐
                        │           GRAPH SCHEMA          │
                        └─────────────────────────────────┘


   ┌──────────────────────┐          ┌──────────────────────┐
   │        User          │          │        Movie         │
   │──────────────────────│          │──────────────────────│
   │  userId   : Integer  │          │  movieId  : Integer  │
   │  gender   : String   │          │  title    : String   │
   │  age      : Integer  │          │  year     : Integer  │
   │  occupation: Integer │          │                      │
   └──────────────────────┘          └──────────────────────┘
              │                                │
              │   [:RATED]                     │  [:HAS_GENRE]
              │   {rating   : Integer (1-5)}   │
              │   {timestamp: Integer (Unix)}  │
              │                                ▼
              │                      ┌──────────────────────┐
              └─────────────────────►│        Genre         │
                                     │──────────────────────│
                                     │  name : String       │
                                     │  e.g. "Action"       │
                                     │       "Comedy"       │
                                     │       "Drama"  ...   │
                                     └──────────────────────┘


  Relationship directions:

  (User) ──[:RATED {rating, timestamp}]──► (Movie)
  (Movie) ──[:HAS_GENRE]──────────────────► (Genre)


  Example traversal:

  (User #1) ──[RATED: 5]──► (Schindler's List)
                                    │
                             [HAS_GENRE]──► (Drama)
                             [HAS_GENRE]──► (War)


1. Які сутності стали вузлами, а які — ребрами? Чому?
What became nodes and what became edges? Why?

Nodes: User, Movie, Genre

These three are independent entities — they exist and have meaning on their own, regardless of any relationship. A user exists even if they haven't rated anything yet. A movie exists even if no one has watched it. A genre like "Drama" exists as a concept independently.

Edges: RATED, HAS_GENRE

These represent relationships between entities, not entities themselves. A rating only exists in the context of a specific user rating a specific movie — it has no standalone identity. The same logic applies to HAS_GENRE: the connection between a movie and its genre is a fact about their relationship, not an independent thing.

The rule of thumb: if you can ask "how many of these exist on their own?" → it's a node. If it only makes sense as a connection between two things → it's an edge.

2. Оцінка користувача за фільм — це ребро (User)-[:RATED]->(Movie) чи окремий вузол (Rating)? Аргументуйте своє рішення. Це не риторичне запитання: в обох підходів є реальні trade-off-и.
Is a rating an edge or a separate node?

Decision: edge — (User)-[:RATED {rating, timestamp}]->(Movie)

Arguments for edge (our choice):


The most common queries are traversals: "what did this user rate?", "who rated this movie?", "find movies rated ≥4 by user X". These are all 1-hop traversals — extremely fast as edges.
A rating has no identity outside the User↔Movie pair. There's no scenario where you query "give me rating #583291" — you always go through a user or a movie.
Storage is more compact — no extra node, no extra two edges to connect it.


Arguments for a separate (Rating) node:


If you needed to model complex rating metadata (e.g. multiple rating dimensions: acting, plot, visuals), a node accommodates that more naturally.
If you wanted to query ratings independently — e.g. "find all 5-star ratings given between 2000 and 2001" — a node with its own index on timestamp might be faster.
Easier to attach future relationships to a rating (e.g. (Rating)-[:FLAGGED_AS]->(Suspicious)).


Conclusion: For this dataset, the edge model is the right trade-off. The rating properties {rating, timestamp} are simple scalars, and all realistic queries go through User or Movie. The performance benefit of direct traversal outweighs the flexibility of a rating node.

3. Чому жанри фільму вигідніше зберігати як окремі вузли (Genre), а не як список у властивості вузла Movie?
Why store genres as separate nodes, not a list property?

If genres were stored as a list on the Movie node:

// With list property — slow, no index possible on list contents
MATCH (m:Movie)
WHERE 'Thriller' IN m.genres
RETURN m

This forces a full scan of every Movie node, checking the list on each one. Neo4j cannot index inside a list property efficiently.

With Genre as a separate node:

cypher// With Genre node — instant lookup via index on Genre.name
MATCH (g:Genre {name: 'Thriller'})<-[:HAS_GENRE]-(m:Movie)
RETURN m

Neo4j finds the single Genre {name: 'Thriller'} node in O(1) via index, then follows its incoming HAS_GENRE edges — only touching the relevant movies.

Additional benefits of Genre nodes:


Enables queries across genres: "find genres whose average rating > 4.0" becomes a simple aggregation.
Genres become first-class citizens you can attach more data to (e.g. Genre {name, description, parentGenre}).
The graph becomes more semantically rich — two movies sharing a Genre node are visibly connected in the graph, enabling genre-based community detection.
